    // Des's per-category copy from the ODS VLOOKUP sheet; fills Extended Description, overrides win
    'category_copy' => [
        'American Innovation Dollar' => ['note' => 'Innovation Dollars, introduced in 2018, highlight American innovations and inventions. Each year features a new theme on the reverse, with the Statue of Liberty on the obverse. Minted in copper-nickel and manganese brass, these dollars pay homage to groundbreaking achievements. Innovation Dollars celebrate the spirit of creativity and progress that defines the United States and reflect the nation\'s ongoing pursuit of innovation across various fields.'],
        'America the Beautiful Quarter' => ['copy' => 'The National Park quarters series honors a national park or historic site in each of the 50 states, the District of Columbia and the five U.S. territories. Each coin bears John Flanagan\'s modified Washington portrait obverse and a special reverse design representing the featured site.', 'note' => 'National Park Quarters, issued since 2010, celebrate America\'s natural and historical treasures. Each coin features a national park or site from each U.S. state on the reverse, while the obverse retains the portrait of George Washington. This ongoing series highlights the nation\'s beauty and heritage, encouraging interest in history and conservation. With unique designs for each park, these quarters offer a tangible connection to America\'s landscapes.'],
        'Barber Dime' => ['copy' => 'Known as the Barber dime after its designer, this classic Liberty Head dime was struck in 90% silver. Its obverse depicts Liberty facing right wearing a Phrygian cap and laurel wreath, while the reverse shows a wreath and the denomination ONE DIME.', 'note' => 'Barber Dimes, struck 1892-1916, symbolize the turn of the century in American coinage. Charles Barber\'s design showcases Lady Liberty wearing a Phrygian cap, while the reverse features an olive wreath and denomination. Barber Dimes offer a tangible link to the dawn of the 20th century, combining history, artistry, and numismatic allure.'],
        'Barber Half Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This 90% silver Liberty Head half dollar (or Barber half dollar) bears Charles E. Barber\'s classic depiction of Liberty wearing a laurel wreath and Phrygian cap held by a band inscribed LIBERTY. The reverse bears an adaptation of the Great Seal of the United States.', 'note' => 'Barber Half Dollars were coined from 1892 to 1915. Charles Barber\'s design features Liberty\'s profile on the obverse, and features an eagle with a shield clutching a bundle of arrows and an olive branch on the reverse. Barber Half Dollars represent a period of transition and change, reflecting the nation\'s growth and the dynamic economic landscape. Their timeless appeal and historical context make them an enduring choice for numismatic enthusiasts.'],
        'Barber Quarter' => ['copy' => 'Designed by Charles E. Barber, this 90% silver Liberty Head quarter (or Barber quarter) depicts Liberty wearing a laurel wreath and Phrygian cap with a band inscribed LIBERTY. The reverse bears an adaptation of the Great Seal of the United States.', 'note' => 'Barber Quarters were struck from 1892 to 1916. Charles Barber\'s design features Liberty with a laurel wreath on the obverse, while the reverse showcases an eagle with a shield and olive branches. These silver quarters reflect an era of technological advancements and societal shifts. Barber Quarters provide collectors with a tangible link to a dynamic period, blending history, artistry, and numismatic allure into a single coin series.'],
        'Braided Hair Half Cent' => ['copy' => 'This scarce copper Braided Hair half cent was struck over 150 years ago. It bears Christian Gobrecht\'s classic design. The obverse depicts Liberty wearing a diadem, her long hair in a bun held by beaded cords. The reverse shows HALF CENTS surrounded by a wreath and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Half Cents, minted 1793-1857, offer a fascinating window into early U.S. coinage. These copper coins, smaller than a modern penny, showcase various designs of Liberty on the obverse and a wreath and denomination on the reverse. Despite their small denomination, Half Cents were vital in everyday commerce, as America was finding its economic footing. Collectors value Half Cents for their their representation of a formative period in U.S. history.'],
        'Braided Hair Large Cent' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 150 years ago, this copper Braided Hair large cent bears Christian Gobrecht\'s classic design. Its obverse depicts Liberty in a diadem, her long tresses held in a bun held by two beaded cords. The reverse bears ONE CENT encircled by a wreath and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'U.S. Large Cents, minted 1793-1857, signify early American coinage. Various designs and types reflect their evolution. Composed of copper, these cents transitioned in design and composition. Large Cents are tangible connections to the young United States, its changing symbolism, and economic shifts. These coins offer insight into America\'s progress and numismatic diversity, showcasing the nation\'s journey from colonial times to the brink of the Civil War.'],
        'Buffalo Nickel' => ['copy' => 'Also called the Indian Head nickel, the prized Buffalo nickel is considered the most uniquely American coin. Designed by sculptor James E. Fraser, the obverse depicts a proud Native American. The distinctive reverse features an American bison.', 'note' => 'Buffalo Nickels, coined from 1913 to 1938, embody the American frontier spirit. Designed by James Earle Fraser, their obverse displays a captivating Native American profile, while the reverse features a realistic depiction of an American bison or buffalo. Composed of copper and nickel, these coins offer a distinct matte finish. The introductory reverse of the buffalo on raised ground caused concerns of rapid wear, leading to design modifications the same year.'],
        'Capped Bust Dime' => ['copy' => 'Designed by John Reich, this classic silver dime depicts Liberty on the obverse with hair beneath a cloth cap surrounded by 13 stars. Its reverse features the denomination 10 c. beneath an eagle with shield on a branch holding three arrows.', 'note' => 'Capped Bust Dimes, minted 1809-1837, epitomize the evolving American coinage. John Reich\'s design replaced the Draped Bust motif, showcasing Lady Liberty with a cap. The reverse features a heraldic eagle. Variations include large and small dates, changes in denticles, and design. Composed of silver, these dimes bridge the gap between early and mid-19th-century coinage, offering collectors a glimpse into the nation\'s transformation during a dynamic period of history.'],
        'Capped Bust Half Dime' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 180 years ago, this silver Capped Bust half dime bears John Reich\'s classic design. It depicts 13 stars around Liberty in a cloth cap, her curly hair reaching her shoulder where a brooch secures her gown. The reverse bears UNITED STATES OF AMERICA around an American eagle with Union Shield holding an olive branch and three arrows. The denomination 5 c. is below the eagle, with E PLURIBUS UNUM on a banner above.', 'note' => 'Half Dimes, minted from 1794 to 1873, hold a unique place in U.S. numismatic history. These small silver coins, featuring intricate designs, predate the dime denomination. Their small size and intricate designs showcase various portayals of liberty, evolving over time. Their reverse displays either an eagle or wreath. These coins provide a tangible link to early American commerce, craftsmanship, and the nation\'s evolution.'],
        'Capped Bust Half Dollar' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 180 years ago, this 89% silver Capped Bust half dollar bears John Reich\'s classic design. It depicts Liberty with long, curling hair and a cloth cap, surrounded by 13 stars and the date. The reverse shows an American eagle with Union Shield, an olive branch and three arrows. A banner above the eagle reads E PLURIBUS UNUM, with 50 c. below and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA surrounding. Inscribed along the coin\'s edge is FIFTY CENTS or HALF A DOLLAR.', 'alt1' => 'This 90% silver Capped Bust half dollar was struck over 180 years ago. Its obverse depicts 13 stars around Liberty, who wears long hair and a cloth cap. The reverse shows an American eagle with shield, olive branch and three arrows. Surrounding the eagle are the denomination and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Capped Bust Half Dollars, minted 1807-1839, encapsulate early 19th-century coinage. John Reich\'s design showcases Lady Liberty with a cap on the obverse, and the reverse features an eagle with a shield holding an olive branch and arrows. Variations include different sizes, design changes, and a lettered or reeded edge. Capped Bust Half Dollars provide collectors a glimpse into the young United States, bridging the gap between the nation\'s founding and its expansion.'],
        'Capped Bust Quarter' => ['copy' => 'Struck around 200 years ago, this silver Capped Bust quarter bears John Reich\'s classic Capped Bust design. It depicts Liberty in a cloth cap, surrounded by 13 stars and the date of issue. The reverse shows a banner with the motto E PLURIBUS UNUM above an eagle with Union Shield, an olive branch and three arrows. Encircling the eagle are 25 c. and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'alt1' => 'Struck over 180 years ago, this Capped Bust silver quarter bears John Reich\'s Capped Bust design, which William Kneass modified. It depicts Liberty in a cloth cap, surrounded by 13 stars and the date of issue. The reverse shows an American eagle with Union Shield, an olive branch and three arrows. Encircling the eagle are 25 c. and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Capped Bust Quarters, minted 1815-1838, represent a transitional period in U.S. coinage. Designed by John Reich, their obverse showcases Lady Liberty wearing a cap, while the reverse features an eagle perched on an olive branch and arrows. Varieties include a large and small diameter, with subtle design changes to the motto and date. Composed of silver, these quarters offer collectors a tangible link to the nation\'s evolution during a dynamic period.'],
        'Classic Gold Commemorative' => ['note' => 'U.S. Commemorative Coins, first minted 1892 and revived in 1982, honor notable events and figures. These distinct coins span various themes, from local celebrations to national milestones. Ranging from silver to gold, these coins capture moments in time, connecting collectors to the nation\'s heritage. U.S. Commemorative Coins are prized for their significance, design, and rarity, serving as both historical artifacts and prized additions to numismatic collections.'],
        'Classic Head Half Cent' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 180 years ago, this scarce copper half cent bears John Reich\'s Classic Head design. The obverse depicts Liberty with curly hair held by a band inscribed LIBERTY. The reverse bears HALF CENT surrounded by a laurel wreath and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Half Cents, minted 1793-1857, offer a fascinating window into early U.S. coinage. These copper coins, smaller than a modern penny, showcase various designs of Liberty on the obverse and a wreath and denomination on the reverse. Despite their small denomination, Half Cents were vital in everyday commerce, as America was finding its economic footing. Collectors value Half Cents for their their representation of a formative period in U.S. history.'],
        'Classic Head Large Cent' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 200 years ago, this copper Classic Head large cent is 50% larger than today\'s cents. Its classic obverse by John Reich depicts 13 stars around Liberty, her curly hair held by a band inscribed LIBERTY. The reverse bears ONE CENT inside a laurel wreath and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'U.S. Large Cents, minted 1793-1857, signify early American coinage. Various designs and types reflect their evolution. Composed of copper, these cents transitioned in design and composition. Large Cents are tangible connections to the young United States, its changing symbolism, and economic shifts. These coins offer insight into America\'s progress and numismatic diversity, showcasing the nation\'s journey from colonial times to the brink of the Civil War.'],
        'Classic Silver Commemorative' => ['note' => 'U.S. Commemorative Coins, first minted 1892 and revived in 1982, honor notable events and figures. These distinct coins span various themes, from local celebrations to national milestones. Ranging from silver to gold, these coins capture moments in time, connecting collectors to the nation\'s heritage. U.S. Commemorative Coins are prized for their significance, design, and rarity, serving as both historical artifacts and prized additions to numismatic collections.'],
        'Coronet Head Large Cent' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 180 years ago, this copper Coronet Head large cent - also called the Matron Head large cent - is 50% larger than today\'s cents. It depicts a more serious Liberty, with a diadem inscribed LIBERTY and long hair in a bun held by plain cords. The reverse shows ONE CENT surrounded by a laurel wreath and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'U.S. Large Cents, minted 1793-1857, signify early American coinage. Various designs and types reflect their evolution. Composed of copper, these cents transitioned in design and composition. Large Cents are tangible connections to the young United States, its changing symbolism, and economic shifts. These coins offer insight into America\'s progress and numismatic diversity, showcasing the nation\'s journey from colonial times to the brink of the Civil War.'],
        'DC & US Territories Quarter' => ['copy' => 'In 2009, the U.S. Mint followed its Statehood quarter program with six new quarters bearing reverse designs honoring the District of Columbia and the five U.S. territories. Each bore John Flanagan\'s modified Washington portrait obverse.'],
        'Draped Bust Dime' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 200 years ago, this classic Draped Bust silver dime designed by Robert Scot depicts Liberty with a plunging neckline and a ribbon in her flowing hair. 13 stars surround her, with LIBERTY above and the date below. The reverse bears a large heraldic eagle patterned after the Great Seal of the United States.', 'note' => 'Draped Bust Dimes, struck 1796-1807, embody the young United States\' numismatic heritage. Designed by Robert Scot, their obverse features Lady Liberty with a flowing drape, while the reverse showcased an eagle perched on a wreath, later changed to a heraldic eagle. These silver coins, marked by artistic elegance, saw various design variations and scarce mintage. Their historical significance and evolving aesthetics make Draped Bust Dimes sought-after collector\'s items.'],
        'Draped Bust Dollar' => ['alt1' => 'This Draped Bust silver dollar was struck over 200 years ago. Its classic obverse by Robert Scot depicts 13 stars around Liberty with a plunging neckline and a ribbon in her long hair. Scot\'s reverse bears a stunning heraldic eagle adapted from the Great Seal of the United States.'],
        'Draped Bust Half Cent' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 200 years ago, this scarce copper half cent bears Robert Scot\'s classic Draped Bust design. The obverse shows Liberty with a ribbon in her flowing hair and a plunging neckline draped with linen. The reverse bears UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and the fraction 1/200 beneath a laurel wreath and HALF CENT.', 'note' => 'Half Cents, minted 1793-1857, offer a fascinating window into early U.S. coinage. These copper coins, smaller than a modern penny, showcase various designs of Liberty on the obverse and a wreath and denomination on the reverse. Despite their small denomination, Half Cents were vital in everyday commerce, as America was finding its economic footing. Collectors value Half Cents for their their representation of a formative period in U.S. history.'],
        'Draped Bust Large Cent' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 200 years ago, this copper Draped Bust large cent is 50% larger than today\'s cents. Its classic obverse by Robert Scot depicts Liberty with a plunging neckline and flowing hair held by a ribbon. The reverse shows ONE CENT, surrounded by a wreath, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and the fraction 1/100.', 'note' => 'U.S. Large Cents, minted 1793-1857, signify early American coinage. Various designs and types reflect their evolution. Composed of copper, these cents transitioned in design and composition. Large Cents are tangible connections to the young United States, its changing symbolism, and economic shifts. These coins offer insight into America\'s progress and numismatic diversity, showcasing the nation\'s journey from colonial times to the brink of the Civil War.'],
        'Draped Bust Quarter' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 200 years ago, this silver Draped Bust quarter bears Robert Scot\'s classic designs. The obverse shows 13 stars around Liberty with a plunging neckline, her long hair held by a ribbon. The reverse is the second design to appear on a U.S. quarter. It bears a heraldic eagle surrounded by UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and the denomination 20 c.', 'note' => 'Draped Bust Quarters, struck 1796-1807, are early American coinage treasures. Their obverse features Lady Liberty\'s elegant profile, hair draped with a ribbon. The reverse showcases an eagle with outstretched wings, clutching arrows and an olive branch. Variations include Small Eagle and Heraldic Eagle types. These silver quarters reflect the nation\'s formative years, offering collectors a tangible connection to its fledgling economy and evolving design aesthetics.'],
        'Eisenhower Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This large dollar coin depicts 34th President Dwight D. Eisenhower, with LIBERTY above and the date below. Next to the portrait is the motto IN GOD WE TRUST. Adapted from the Apollo 11 mission patch, the reverse shows an eagle landing on the moon\'s surface, with the earth and E PLURIBUS UNUM above. Encircling the design are UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and ONE DOLLAR.', 'alt1' => 'This special Bicentennial dollar was struck to celebrate the 200th anniversary of America\'s founding. Its obverse depicts 34th President Dwight D. Eisenhower and the dual date 1776-1976. On the reverse, the Liberty Bell is superimposed over the moon, with E PLURIBUS UNUM to the right. UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and ONE DOLLAR encircle the reverse.', 'note' => 'Eisenhower Dollars were minted from 1971 to 1978. Designed by Frank Gasparro, they feature Eisenhower on the obverse and an eagle landing on the Moon on the reverse. Minted in 40% silver, these dollars capture the nation\'s space achievements and spirit. Eisenhower Dollars serve as reminders of an era defined by exploration and cultural change. Collectors appreciate their link to both presidential history and the space race, making them sought-after numismatic pieces.'],
        'Flowing Hair Half Dime' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 200 years ago, this silver Flowing Hair half dime is one of America\'s oldest federal-issue coins. Its obverse bears Robert Scot\'s classic image of Liberty with long hair flowing to her shoulders. Surrounding her are 13 stars, with the date below. The reverse depicts an American eagle encircled by a laurel wreath and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Half Dimes, minted from 1794 to 1873, hold a unique place in U.S. numismatic history. These small silver coins, featuring intricate designs, predate the dime denomination. Their small size and intricate designs showcase various portayals of liberty, evolving over time. Their reverse displays either an eagle or wreath. These coins provide a tangible link to early American commerce, craftsmanship, and the nation\'s evolution.'],
        'Flying Eagle Small Cent' => ['copy' => 'Briefly minted from 1856-1858, Flying Eagle cents were America\'s first small-size 1c coins. Struck in 88% copper and 12% nickel, these coins feature an eagle in flight on the obverse and a wreath of corn, wheat, cotton and tobacco on the reverse.', 'note' => 'The Flying Eagle Cent, minted from 1856, featured a detailed flying eagle on the smaller copper-nickel coin, a departure from large cents. Designed by James B. Longacre, it marked a shift in coinage. Despite wear issues, it\'s an iconic piece transitioning to the Indian Head Cent in 1859, holding immense numismatic value.'],
        'Fractional Pioneer Gold' => ['copy' => 'Struck during the California gold rush, this privately minted gold piece helped ease the shortage of small-denomination coins. Containing up to 85% of face value in real, California gold, these unique and historic issues quickly became collector favorites.'],
        'Franklin Half Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This Franklin half dollar is from America\'s last circulating half dollar series struck entirely in 90% silver. Its obverse bears John R. Sinnock\'s portrait of Benjamin Franklin, while the reverse depicts the Liberty Bell and a small eagle.', 'note' => 'Franklin Half Dollars, minted 1948-1963, reflect the mid-20th century American character. John Sinnock\'s design showcases Benjamin Franklin on the obverse, while the reverse displays the Liberty Bell. These half dollars have no variations, making them popular among collectors seeking a cohesive series. Franklin Half Dollars capture the nation\'s post-war optimism. Their historical context make them sought-after numismatic pieces, representing a dynamic period in U.S. history.'],
        'Gold $2.50 Quarter Eagle' => ['copy' => 'This gold Classic Head quarter eagle was designed by William Kneass. Its obverse is based on John Reich\'s classic image of Liberty with flowing hair held by a band inscribed LIBERTY. The date is below, with 13 stars surrounding. The reverse depicts UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and 2 1/2 D. around an American eagle with Union shield, olive branch and three arrows.', 'alt1' => 'This gold Liberty Head quarter eagle - also called a Coronet Head or Braided Hair quarter eagle - bears Christian Gobrecht\'s classic designs. The obverse depicts 13 stars around Liberty, who wears a diadem inscribed LIBERTY. A string of beads holds her hair up in a bun. The reverse shows an American eagle with Union Shield, oak branch and three arrows. Encircling the design are united STATES OF AMERICA and 2 1/2 D.', 'alt2' => 'This gold Indian Head quarter eagle is one of America\'s most unique coins. Designed by Bela Lyon Pratt, the designs are sunken into the surface. The obverse depicts a Native American chief surrounded by 13 stars, with LIBERTY above and the date below. The reverse shows an eagle with olive branch perched on a bundle of arrows, encircled by the inscriptions E PLURIBUS UNUM, IN GOD WE TRUST, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and 2 1/2 DOLLARS.'],
        'Gold $3' => ['copy' => 'This Indian Princess three-dollar gold piece was designed by James B. Longacre. His classic obverse depicts Liberty in a crown of feathers, on which is a band inscribed LIBERTY. Surrounding her is the inscription UNITED STATES OF AMERICA. The reverse features a wreath of corn, wheat, cotton and tobacco around 3 DOLLARS and the date of issue.'],
        'Gold $5 Half Eagle' => ['copy' => 'This gold Classic Head half eagle was designed by William Kneass. Its obverse is based on John Reich\'s classic image of Liberty with flowing hair held by a band inscribed LIBERTY. The date is below, with 13 stars surrounding. The reverse depicts UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and 2 1/2 D. around an American eagle with Union shield, olive branch and three arrows.', 'alt1' => 'This gold Liberty Head half eagle - also called a Coronet Head or Braided Hair half eagle - bears Christian Gobrecht\'s classic designs. The obverse depicts 13 stars around Liberty, who wears a diadem and her hair in a bun held by a string of beads. On the reverse, an American eagle with Union Shield holds an olive branch and three arrows. The denomination FIVE D. and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA encircle the reverse.', 'alt2' => 'This gold Liberty Head half eagle - also called a Coronet Head or Braided Hair half eagle - bears Christian Gobrecht\'s classic designs. The obverse depicts 13 stars around Liberty, who wears a diadem and her long hair in a bun held by a string of beads. On the reverse, an American eagle with Union Shield holds an olive branch and three arrows. Above the eagle is a banner with IN GOD WE TRUST, while UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and FIVE D. encircle the reverse.'],
        'Gold $10 Eagle' => ['copy' => 'This gold Liberty Head eagle - also called a Coronet Head or Braided Hair eagle - bears Christian Gobrecht\'s classic designs. The obverse shows 13 stars around Liberty, who wears a diadem and her long hair in a bun held by a string of beads. On the reverse, a proud American eagle with Union Shield holds an olive branch and three arrows. The denomination TEN D. and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA encircle the reverse.', 'alt1' => 'This gold Liberty Head eagle - also called a Coronet Head or Braided Hair eagle - bears Christian Gobrecht\'s classic designs. The obverse depicts 13 stars around Liberty, who wears a diadem inscribed liberty. A string of beads holds her hair up in a bun. The reverse features an American eagle with Union Shield, an olive branch and three arrows. IN GOD WE TRUST appears on a banner above the eagle, with UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and FIVE D. encircling the design.', 'alt2' => 'This gold Indian Head eagle is one of America\'s most beautiful coins. Its obverse depicts Liberty crowned in a Native American war bonnet, with 13 stars above and the date below. On the reverse, an American perches atop a bundle of arrows and an olive branch. Surrounding the eagle are E PLURIBUS UNUM, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and TEN DOLLARS. Along the edge are 46 stars - one for each state in the union when the coin was struck.'],
        'Gold $20 Double Eagle' => ['copy' => 'Struck in 90% gold over a century ago, this gold Liberty Head double eagle is larger than a Kennedy half dollar and weighs over 1 troy ounce. It bears James B. Longacre\'s classic design. The obverse shows Liberty in a diadem with her long hair up in a bun, surrounded by 13 stars and the date of issue. The reverse bears a stylish heraldic eagle beneath a circle of 13 stars and series of rays. The denomination and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA encircle the design.'],
        'Gold Bullion Coin' => ['copy' => 'This hefty $5 American Buffalo gold bullion coin contains 1/10 ounce of 99.99% pure gold. Only issued in 2008, it\'s from the first 24K gold coin series produced by the U.S. Mint. The coin bears James E. Fraser\'s iconic Buffalo nickel (or Indian Head) nickel designs. The obverse depicts a proud Native American, while the reverse shows an American bison.', 'alt1' => 'This hefty $10 American Buffalo gold bullion coin contains 1/4 ounce of 99.99% pure gold. Only issued in 2008, it\'s from the first 24K gold coin series produced by the U.S. Mint. The coin bears James E. Fraser\'s iconic Buffalo nickel (or Indian Head) nickel designs. The obverse depicts a proud Native American, while the reverse shows an American bison.', 'alt2' => 'This hefty $25 American Buffalo gold bullion coin contains 1/2 ounce of 99.99% pure gold. Only issued in 2008, it\'s from the first 24K gold coin series produced by the U.S. Mint. The coin bears James E. Fraser\'s iconic Buffalo nickel (or Indian Head) nickel designs. The obverse depicts a proud Native American, while the reverse shows an American bison.'],
        'Indian Head Small Cent' => ['copy' => 'Struck from 1859-1864, copper-nickel Indian Head cents depict Liberty with an Indian headdress and not an actual Native American. The reverse of first-year coins featured a laurel wreath, which changed in 1860 to an oak wreath and small shield.', 'alt1' => 'The composition of Indian Head cents was changed to bronze during 1864, and a slightly sharper Indian Head portrait was created near the end of 1864 that included designer James B. Longacre\'s initial L on the lower ribbon behind the neck.', 'note' => 'Struck from 1859-1864, copper-nickel Indian Head cents depict Liberty with an Indian headdress and not an actual Native American. The reverse of first-year coins featured a laurel wreath, which changed in 1860 to an oak wreath and small shield. A slightly sharper Indian portrait was created near the end of 1864 and included designer James B. Longacre\'s initial L on the lower ribbon behind the neck. Indian Heads were struck in bronze through the end of the series in 1909.'],
        'Jefferson Nickel' => ['copy' => 'The Jefferson nickel offers collectors an affordable way to build a complete collection. The obverse bears a striking portrait of America\'s founding father and 3rd president, while the reverse depicts his home, Monticello.', 'alt1' => 'Unlike traditional Jefferson nickels, this historic coin was struck in a special alloy of 35% silver to save nickel for the World War II effort. Like all silver wartime nickels, it bears a larger mint mark on the reverse above Monticello\'s dome.', 'alt2' => 'This coin is the first of four issues in the Westward Journey nickel series honoring the bicentennial of the Louisiana Purchase and the Lewis & Clark expedition. Its one-year-only design bears a special reverse adapted from the Indian Peace medals carried by Lewis and Clark.', 'note' => 'Jefferson Nickels, minted in a copper-nickel composition since 1938, reflect a modern era in U.S. coinage. The obverse showcases a thoughtful portrait of Thomas Jefferson, while the reverse, featured his Monticello home. A notable variation is the Wartime silver alloy nickels, used from 1942 to 1945 due to wartime nickel shortages. The iconic 2003 "Westward Journey" series explored Jefferson\'s expeditions with two unique obverses and four distinct reverses.'],
        'Kennedy Half Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This Kennedy half dollar honors America\'s 35th president, John F. Kennedy. The obverse designed by Gilroy Roberts features a left-facing portrait of Kennedy, while Frank Gasparro\'s reverse displays the presidential coat of arms.', 'alt1' => 'This Bicentennial half dollar honors the 200th anniversary of America\'s founding with Seth Huntington\'s reverse featuring Independence Hall and the inscription 200 YEARS OF FREEDOM. The obverse by Gilroy Roberts depicts 35th President John F. Kennedy and the dual date 1776-1976.', 'note' => 'Kennedy Half Dollars, minted since 1964, honor President John F. Kennedy\'s legacy. Gilroy Roberts and Frank Gasparro\'s design showcases Kennedy\'s portrait on the obverse, while the reverse features the presidential seal. Initially silver, the composition shifted to copper-nickel in 1971. Varieties include proof sets, mintmarks, and special editions. Kennedy Half Dollars are a poignant link to the 1960s and the nation\'s collective memory of JFK.'],
        'Liberty Cap Half Cent' => ['note' => 'Half Cents, minted 1793-1857, offer a fascinating window into early U.S. coinage. These copper coins, smaller than a modern penny, showcase various designs of Liberty on the obverse and a wreath and denomination on the reverse. Despite their small denomination, Half Cents were vital in everyday commerce, as America was finding its economic footing. Collectors value Half Cents for their their representation of a formative period in U.S. history.'],
        'Liberty Cap Large Cent' => ['note' => 'U.S. Large Cents, minted 1793-1857, signify early American coinage. Various designs and types reflect their evolution. Composed of copper, these cents transitioned in design and composition. Large Cents are tangible connections to the young United States, its changing symbolism, and economic shifts. These coins offer insight into America\'s progress and numismatic diversity, showcasing the nation\'s journey from colonial times to the brink of the Civil War.'],
        'Liberty Nickel' => ['copy' => 'When first issued in 1883, Liberty Head Nickels (or "V" nickels) bore just a large Roman numeral "V" for the denomination. So the Mint soon added the word CENTS to stop con artists from gold plating them and passing them off as $5 gold pieces.', 'note' => 'Liberty Head Nickels, struck from 1883 to 1913, hold a special place in U.S. coinage history. Known as "V" nickels due to the Roman numeral "V" on the reverse, they feature the iconic Liberty portrait on the obverse. The introduction of this design led to controversy, as the initial version lacked the word "CENT," sparking confusion. Composed of copper and nickel, these coins combine artistic elegance with practical utility.'],
        'Liberty Walking Half Dollar' => ['copy' => 'Designed by Adolph A. Weinman, this 90% silver Liberty Walking half dollar is one of America\'s most beautiful coins. The obverse shows Liberty with arm outstretched, striding towards the dawn of a new day. The reverse depicts an eagle on a rocky crag, where a pine branch grows to symbolize strength.', 'note' => 'Walking Liberty Half Dollars were minted from 1916-1947. Adolph A. Weinman\'s design showcases Liberty striding confidently on the obverse, while the reverse displays an eagle with outstretched wings. Composed of silver, these half dollars are tied to WWI and WII eras. Walking Liberty Half Dollars symbolize the nation\'s enduring spirit during challenging times. Collectors admire their historical significance and the link they provide to pivotal moments in U.S. history.'],
        'Lincoln Bicentennial Small Cent' => ['copy' => 'In honor of the bicentennial of Abraham Lincoln\'s birth and the 100th anniversary of the Lincoln cent, this special one-year-only cent bears a special reverse design depicting the first of four major stages in Abraham Lincoln\'s life.', 'alt1' => 'In honor of the bicentennial of Abraham Lincoln\'s birth and the 100th anniversary of the Lincoln cent, this special one-year-only cent bears a special reverse design depicting the second of four major stages in Abraham Lincoln\'s life.', 'alt2' => 'In honor of the bicentennial of Abraham Lincoln\'s birth and the 100th anniversary of the Lincoln cent, this special one-year-only cent bears a special reverse design depicting the third of four major stages in Abraham Lincoln\'s life.', 'note' => 'The Lincoln Bicentennial Cents, issued during 2009, celebrate Abraham Lincoln\'s 200th birthday and the first issue of the Lincoln cent. Four unique reverse designs depict his life stages: birthplace, formative years, professional life, and presidency. These coins honor his lasting impact on the nation. The obverse retains the classic profile. Struck in copper alloy, they\'re a captivating addition for collectors, reflecting history and artistry during the bicentennial years.'],
        'Lincoln Memorial Small Cent' => ['copy' => 'The Lincoln cent was redesigned in 1959 to celebrate the 150th anniversary of Abraham Lincoln\'s birth. The obverse continued to depict Lincoln\'s portrait, while Frank Gasparro\'s new reverse depicted the Lincoln Memorial.', 'note' => 'The Lincoln Memorial Cent, minted from 1959 to 2008, is a cherished numismatic gem. Honoring Abraham Lincoln\'s 150th birth anniversary, its obverse showcases a refined profile of the revered President. The reverse depicts the iconic Lincoln Memorial, a symbol of national unity. In 1982, the coin underwent a composition change from copper alloy to copper-plated zinc. Its long mintage period and historical significance make the Lincoln Memorial Cent a favorite among collectors.'],
        'Lincoln Shield Small Cent' => ['copy' => 'Lincoln cents issued 2010-date bear a new reverse by Lyndall Bass, which depicts a "Union Shield" to represent President Lincoln\'s preservation of the United States as a single, unified nation. The obverse bears Victor D. Brenner\'s original portrait design.', 'note' => 'The Lincoln Shield Cents, introduced in 2010, mark a contemporary chapter in the Lincoln Cent series. The obverse features the familiar profile of Abraham Lincoln, paying homage to tradition. The reverse showcases a distinctive shield emblem, representing Lincoln\'s dedication to preserving the Union. Struck in a copper-plated zinc composition, these cents offer durability and affordability for collectors while still honoring the enduring legacy of a revered President.'],
        'Lincoln Wheat Small Cent' => ['copy' => 'Lincoln cents with the original Wheat Ears reverse were introduced in 1909 on the 100th anniversary of Abraham Lincoln\'s birth and were struck until 1958. These bronze cents were the first circulating U.S. coins to depict an actual person.', 'alt1' => 'In 1943, the U.S. Mint struck Lincoln cents in zinc-coated steel to save copper for munitions and other military materials in World War II. Each unique one-year-only issue bears Victor D. Brenner\'s Lincoln portrait obverse and Wheat Ears reverse designs.', 'note' => 'Lincoln cents with the original Wheat Ears reverse were introduced in 1909 on the 100th anniversary of Abraham Lincoln\'s birth and were struck until 1958. These bronze cents were the first circulating U.S. coins to feature a portrait of a historical figure. Over its decades-long circulation, the Lincoln Wheat Cent only underwent one composition change. In 1943, the composition was changed from bronze to zinc-coated steel to save copper during the war.'],
        'Mercury Dime' => ['copy' => 'This 90% silver Mercury dime was designed by A.A. Weinman, who also created the Liberty Walking half dollars of 1916-1947. Its obverse features Liberty in a winged cap to symbolize freedom of thought. The reverse depicts a fasces against a branch in the background.', 'note' => 'Mercury Dimes, minted 1916-1945, captivate collectors with their iconic design. Created by A.A. Weinman, these silver dimes feature Liberty wearing a winged cap, often mistaken for Mercury, the Roman god. The reverse showcases a fasces and olive branch, symbolizing strength and peace. Mercury Dimes gained popularity due to their timeless beauty and connection to World War I and II eras. They serve as enduring reminders of an eventful period in U.S. history and numismatics.'],
        'Mint Set' => ['note' => 'U.S. Mint Sets, offered since the 1940s, compile uncirculated coins in one package. These sets include coins from the Denver and Philadelphia Mint. Mint Sets cater to both collectors and hobbyists, providing a comprehensive snapshot of annual coinage. With their sealed packaging and careful handling, U.S. Mint Sets offer coins in pristine condition. These sets provide a hassle-free entry into coin collecting, combining historical value with numismatic intrigue.'],
        'Modern Gold Commemorative' => ['note' => 'U.S. Commemorative Coins, first minted 1892 and revived in 1982, honor notable events and figures. These distinct coins span various themes, from local celebrations to national milestones. Ranging from silver to gold, these coins capture moments in time, connecting collectors to the nation\'s heritage. U.S. Commemorative Coins are prized for their significance, design, and rarity, serving as both historical artifacts and prized additions to numismatic collections.'],
        'Modern Silver/Clad Commemorative' => ['note' => 'U.S. Commemorative Coins, first minted 1892 and revived in 1982, honor notable events and figures. These distinct coins span various themes, from local celebrations to national milestones. Ranging from silver to gold, these coins capture moments in time, connecting collectors to the nation\'s heritage. U.S. Commemorative Coins are prized for their significance, design, and rarity, serving as both historical artifacts and prized additions to numismatic collections.'],
        'Morgan Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This large 90% silver Morgan dollar bears George T. Morgan\'s classic depiction of Liberty facing left in a Phrygian cap and a spray of leaves held by a ribbon inscribed LIBERTY. The reverse features an eagle clutching a branch and three arrows.', 'note' => 'Morgan Silver Dollars were minted in the United States from 1878 to 1904, and again in 1921. Named after the designer, George T. Morgan, these coins feature Lady Liberty on the obverse and an eagle with outstretched wings on the reverse. Many were minted in various locations, such as Carson City (CC), New Orleans (O), San Francisco (S), and Philadelphia (no mint mark). Today, Morgan Dollars are popular among collectors due to their historical significance and artistic appeal.'],
        'Morgan Dollar Toned' => ['copy' => 'This large 90% silver Morgan dollar bears George T. Morgan\'s classic depiction of Liberty facing left in a Phrygian cap and a spray of leaves held by a ribbon inscribed LIBERTY. The reverse features an eagle clutching a branch and three arrows.', 'note' => 'Morgan Silver Dollars were minted in the United States from 1878 to 1904, and again in 1921. Named after the designer, George T. Morgan, these coins feature Lady Liberty on the obverse and an eagle with outstretched wings on the reverse. Many were minted in various locations, such as Carson City (CC), New Orleans (O), San Francisco (S), and Philadelphia (no mint mark). Today, Morgan Dollars are popular among collectors due to their historical significance and artistic appeal.'],
        'Native American Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This historic Sacagawea dollar honors Lewis and Clark\'s young Shoshone guide. The obverse depicts Sacagawea holding her infant son, Jean Baptiste. LIBERTY is inscribed above the pair, with IN GOD WE TRUST on the left and the date at right. The reverse bears a soaring American eagle and E PLURIBUS UNUM. Encircling the reverse are 13 stars, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and ONE DOLLAR.', 'alt1' => 'This historic Native American dollar is from America\'s third "golden dollar" series. Its obverse depicts Lewis & Clark\'s guide Sacagawea and her infant son, Jean Baptiste. The one-year-only reverse honors Native Americans and their contributions. Inscribed along the edge are the date of issue, mint mark and E PLURIBUS UNUM.', 'note' => 'Sacagawea or Native American Dollars, coined since 2000, honor the Shoshone guide of the Lewis and Clark Expedition. Glenna Goodacre\'s design portrays Sacagawea on the obverse, while the reverse features an eagle in flight. Minted in copper with manganese brass, these dollars represent Native American contributions to U.S. history. Native American Dollars celebrate cultural diversity and historical exploration, fostering awareness of indigenous heritage.'],
        'Peace Dollar' => ['copy' => 'Designed by Anthony de Francisci, this 90% silver Peace depicts a left-facing Liberty wearing a diadem of spikes. The reverse shows an eagle perched on a rock with a laurel branch above the word PEACE, facing the rays of a rising sun.', 'note' => 'Peace Dollars, which minted from 1921 to 1935, symbolize post-World War I optimism. Anthony de Francisci\'s design features Lady Liberty on the obverse and a perched eagle on the reverse. Minted in silver, these dollars reflect a time of reconciliation and global stability. Peace Dollars are numismatic tributes to a world seeking harmony after conflict. These coins capture the spirit of peace and progress during the early 20th century.'],
        'Presidential Dollar' => ['copy' => 'Presidential dollars honor America\'s presidents in order of service. Each obverse depicts the featured president, while a common reverse shows the Statue of Liberty and the denomination $1. Edge inscriptions bear each coin\'s date, mint mark and the mottoes IN GOD WE TRUST and E PLURIBUS UNUM.', 'alt1' => 'Presidential dollars honor America\'s presidents in order of service. Each obverse depicts the featured president, while the reverse shows the Statue of Liberty and the denomination $1. Edge inscriptions bear each coin\'s date, mint mark and the motto E PLURIBUS UNUM.', 'note' => 'Presidential Dollars, coined 2007-2016, honor U.S. Presidents with a series of distinctive portraits. Each year features a different President on the obverse, along with their years in office. The reverse showcases an image of the Statue of Liberty. Minted in copper-nickel and gold-colored manganese brass, these dollars provide a unique historical overview of American leadership. Presidential Dollars offer collectors an engaging journey through America\'s presidential history.'],
        'Proof Set' => ['note' => 'U.S. Proof Sets, introduced in 1936, showcase coins with mirror-like finishes and sharp details. These sets encompass a range of denominations, offering collectors pristine examples of annual coinage. Struck using special dies and polished blanks. Proof Sets provide a glimpse into each year\'s design and minting craftsmanship. U.S. Proof Sets are prized for their aesthetic beauty and numismatic significance, serving as showcases of the U.S. Mint\'s artistry.'],
        'Roosevelt Dime' => ['copy' => 'This dime honors America\'s 32nd and only four-term president, Franklin D. Roosevelt, Designed by John R. Sinnock, the obverse depicts Roosevelt\'s portrait. The reverse shows a torch between olive and oak branches, symbolizing peace, strength and independence.', 'note' => 'Roosevelt Dimes, coined since 1946, honor President Franklin D. Roosevelt\'s legacy. John R. Sinnock\'s design features a dignified profile of Roosevelt on the obverse, while the reverse showcases a torch, olive branch, and oak branch, symbolizing liberty, peace, and strength. Originally composed of silver, these dimes transitioned to copper-nickel alloy in 1965. Roosevelt Dimes hold cultural significance, representing a time of post-war rebuilding and change in America.'],
        'Seated Half Dime' => ['copy' => 'This century-old, 90% silver Liberty Seated half dime is from the final series before the denomination was retired. Its obverse depicts Liberty seated on a rock holding a Union Shield and a Liberty cap atop a pole. The reverse bears HALF DIME surrounded by a laurel wreath and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Half Dimes, minted from 1794 to 1873, hold a unique place in U.S. numismatic history. These small silver coins, featuring intricate designs, predate the dime denomination. Their small size and intricate designs showcase various portayals of liberty, evolving over time. Their reverse displays either an eagle or wreath. These coins provide a tangible link to early American commerce, craftsmanship, and the nation\'s evolution.'],
        'Seated Liberty Dime' => ['copy' => 'Designed by Christian Gobrecht, this 90% silver dime depicts Liberty seated on a rock, holding a shield in her right hand and a Liberty cap on a pole in her left. The reverse displays a wreath, the denomination and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Liberty Seated Dimes, designed by Christian Gobrecht, were minted from 1837-1891. The obverse and reverse designs underwent various slight modifications. These silver dimes had several weight changes symbolized by arrows at date varieties. Collectors are drawn to their historical evolution, reflecting the nation\'s growth during the 19th century. Liberty Seated Dimes encapsulate a tangible connection to a pivotal era in U.S. coinage history.'],
        'Seated Liberty Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This century-old 90% silver Liberty Seated dollar bears Christian Gobrecht\'s classic design. On the obverse, Liberty sits on a rock surrounded by 13 stars. She holds a Union Shield with banner that reads LIBERTY and a Liberty cap atop a pole. The reverse bears an American eagle with shield, olive branch and 3 arrows. Encircling the eagle are the denomination ONE DOL. and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Liberty Seated Dollars, minted 1840-1873, encapsulate mid-19th century America. Christian Gobrecht\'s design showcases Liberty seated on the obverse, while the reverse features an eagle with a shield clutching an olive branch and bundle of arrows. They offer a tangible link to the nation\'s growth, Civil War era, and post-war reconciliation. Liberty Seated Dollars are numismatic relics of a pivotal period, connecting collectors to a time of change, strife, and eventual healing.'],
        'Seated Liberty Half Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This 90% silver Liberty Seated half dollar was struck over a century ago. Its classic obverse shows Liberty seated on a rock, holding a Union Shield, a ribbon inscribed LIBERTY and a Liberty cap atop a pole. 13 stars and the date surround her. The reverse depicts an American eagle with Union Shield, an olive branch and three arrows. Encircling the reverse are UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and HALF DOLLAR.', 'note' => 'Liberty Seated Half Dollars, minted 1839-1891, capture the mid to late 19th-century American spirit. Christian Gobrecht\'s design showcases Liberty seated on the obverse, while the reverse displays an eagle with a shield and olive branches. Varieties include arrows, rays, and motto changes. Composed of silver, these half dollars reflect an evolving nation through the pre-Civil War era and beyond. Liberty Seated Half Dollars offer collectors a tangible connection to history.'],
        'Seated Liberty Quarter' => ['copy' => 'Designed by Christian Gobrecht, this classic 90% silver quarter features Liberty seated on a rock surrounded by 13 stars. Her left hand holds a liberty cap atop a pole; her right, a shield inscribed LIBERTY. The reverse depicts an eagle on an olive branch holding three arrows.', 'note' => 'Liberty Seated Quarters, minted from 1838 to 1891, were designed by Christian Gobrecht. Their obverse features Liberty seated, surrounded by stars, while the reverse showcases an eagle with a shield, olive branches and arrows. Varieties include No Motto and Arrows, reflecting monetary changes. These silver quarters encapsulate the nation\'s growth, from the early 19th century to post-Civil War years. Collectors admire their historical context and intricate designs.'],
        'Shield Nickel' => ['copy' => 'The classic Shield nickel of 1866-1883 replaced the silver half dimes of 1794-1873. The coin\'s obverse depicts a shield and wreath, while the reverse features a large numeral "5" inside a circle of 13 stars above the word CENTS.', 'note' => 'Shield Nickels, minted from 1866 to 1883, offer a fascinating glimpse into post-Civil War U.S. coinage. Composed of a copper-nickel alloy, these coins bear the Union Shield to symbolize unity and expansion. The reverse showcases the numeral "5" encircled by stars. Despite wear issues due to their extensive circulation, Shield Nickels remain a favorite among collectors for their historical significance and unique design, bridging the gap between war and reconstruction periods.'],
        'Silver Bullion Coin' => ['copy' => 'Struck in 1 troy ounce of 99.9% pure silver, this hefty American silver eagle bears the series\' original designs. The obverse bears Adolph A. Weinman\'s beloved image of Liberty striding to greet the dawn of a new day. She wears a billowing gown of stars and stripes, and carries oak and laurel branches for military and civic glory. John Mercanti\'s striking reverse shows a heraldic eagle under a triangle of 13 stars. Encircling the reverse are UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and 1 OZ. FINE SILVER-ONE DOLLAR.', 'note' => 'The American Silver Eagle, since 1986, is a premier silver bullion coin. Adorning Walking Liberty on the obverse and an eagle on the reverse, this coin is struck in one troy ounce of .999 fine silver. Recognized for its purity and iconic design, it\'s a favorite among investors and collectors alike. American Silver Eagles hold enduring value due to their silver content and numismatic appeal, combining precious metal investment with artistic craftsmanship.'],
        'Standing Liberty Quarter' => ['copy' => 'Considered one of America\'s most beautiful coinage designs, this Standing Liberty quarter depicts Liberty standing in a gateway, holding an olive branch and uncovering a shield. The reverse features 13 stars surrounding a majestic eagle in flight.', 'note' => 'Standing Liberty Quarters, minted 1916-1930, symbolize a transformative period. Hermon A. MacNeil\'s design features Liberty on the obverse, modified from exposed to covered. The reverse showcases an eagle in flight. Changes were made to the date to enhance durability. Composed of silver, these quarters embody the nation\'s journey through WWI and into the Roaring Twenties. Collectors value their historical context, striking beauty, and the depiction of a changing America.'],
        'State Quarter' => ['copy' => 'The Statehood quarters of 1999-2008 honored the 50 states in order of statehood, with reverse designs representing the featured state. John Flanagan\'s original Washington portrait obverse was slightly modified to make room for the new, changing reverse designs.', 'note' => 'State Quarters, issued 1999-2008, form a captivating series. Celebrating each U.S. state, their obverse features George Washington, while the reverse showcases unique state symbols and landmarks. This program revitalized coin collecting, sparking widespread interest. State Quarters not only honor America\'s diversity but also connect collectors to local history and culture. Their broad appeal and educational value make them an engaging addition for enthusiasts of all ages.'],
        'Susan B Anthony Dollar' => ['copy' => 'This historic coin is from the America\'s first small-size dollar series. Its obverse depicts women\'s rights pioneer Susan B. Anthony. Surrounding her portrait are by 13 stars, LIBERTY, IN GOD WE TRUST and the date. Adapted from the Apollo 11 mission patch, the reverse shows an eagle landing on the moon with the earth in the background. Above the eagle are UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and E PLURIBUS UNUM, with ONE DOLLAR below.', 'note' => 'Susan B. Anthony Dollars, minted 1979-1981 and in 1999, pay tribute to the women\'s suffrage movement. Their design, by Frank Gasparro, features Anthony on the obverse and an eagle landing on the Moon on the reverse. Composed of copper-nickel, these dollars were smaller than predecessors. Susan B. Anthony Dollars honor a pioneering advocate for gender equality. Despite a brief mintage, they hold historical significance as the first U.S. coin to feature a non-mythical woman.'],
        'Three Cent Nickel' => ['copy' => 'Struck over 130 years ago, this nickel three-cent piece bears James B. Longacre\'s classic design. It depicts Liberty in a diadem inscribed LIBERTY, her long hair in an intricate braid. The reverse bears the Roman numeral III inside by a laurel wreath.', 'note' => 'Nickel Three-Cent Pieces, minted from 1865 to 1889, epitomize 19th-century monetary innovation. These unique coins, composed of a copper-nickel alloy, feature a portrait of liberty on the obverse and a numeral "III" surrounded by a wreath on the reverse. Introduced after the Civil War, they aimed to facilitate commerce in the absence of silver coins.'],
        'Three Cent Silver' => ['copy' => 'This century-old silver three-cent piece (or "Trime") is the smallest-denomination silver coin ever issued by the U.S. Mint. Its obverse depicts a Union Shield atop a six-pointed star. UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and the date encircle the design. The reverse features the Roman numeral III surrounded by a large, stylized letter C and 13 stars.', 'note' => 'Trimes, minted from 1851 to 1873, are a captivating series of tiny U.S. coins. These unique coins, composed of silver, feature a distinctive design with a six-pointed star on the obverse and a Roman numeral III on the reverse. With a diameter slightly larger than a modern dime, they faced challenges due to their small size and easily wore in circulation. Despite their relatively short existence, Trimes intrigue collectors with their rarity and historical significance.'],
        'Trade Dollar' => ['copy' => 'Designed by William Barber, this 90% silver Trade dollar was struck over a century ago for use in trade with Asia. Its obverse depicts 13 stars around Liberty, who sits on a bale of merchandise by the sea. Her right hand extends an olive branch; her left rests atop a sheaf of wheat and a ribbon inscribed LIBERTY. The reverse shows an American eagle holding three arrows and an olive branch. Below the eagle is 420 GRAINS, 900 FINE., while a banner above reads E PLURIBUS UNUM. UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and TRADE DOLLAR encircle the reverse.', 'note' => 'Trade Dollars, minted 1873-1885, bridge U.S. commerce with the Far East. These silver coins, not intended for domestic use, feature Lady Liberty on the obverse and an eagle with spread wings on the reverse. Trade Dollars aimed to facilitate trade in Asian markets, notably China. Varieties include mintmarks and design modifications. Although not widely circulated, they\'re numismatic treasures reflecting America\'s expanding global reach during the late 19th century.'],
        'Twenty Cent' => ['copy' => 'The Liberty Seated twenty-cent piece was the shortest-lived U.S. coin denomination. This century-old, 90% silver coin depicts 13 stars around Liberty, who sits on a rock with a Union Shield and a Liberty cap on a pole. The reverse shows an eagle with an olive branch and three arrows. Encircling the reverse are UNITED STATES OF AMERICA and TWENTY CENTS.', 'note' => 'Twenty Cent Pieces, coined 1875-1878, are intriguing numismatic treasures. Designed by William Barber, these silver coins display a Liberty seated on the obverse, surrounded by stars. The reverse showcases an eagle clutching arrows and olive branches. Despite low circulation due to their similarity to quarters, Twenty Cent Pieces offer a glimpse into the 19th-century U.S. monetary landscape and collectors value their rarity and historical context.'],
        'Two Cent' => ['copy' => 'This century-old bronze two-cent piece is from the first U.S. coin series to bear IN GOD WE TRUST. The motto appears on the obverse, on a ribbon above the Union Shield. Behind the shield are two crossed arrows and an encircling wreath. The reverse bears 2 CENTS surrounded by two sheaves of wheat tied by a ribbon and UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.', 'note' => 'Two-cent pieces, minted from 1864 to 1873, hold a unique place in numismatic history. Introduced due to Civil War-era coin shortages, the obverse portrays a patriotic shield, symbolizing the nation\'s unity during tumultuous times. On the reverse, these coins display an iconic wreath encircling the "2 cents". Despite a short mintage period, these coins intrigue collectors with their historical context and intricate design.'],
        'Washington Quarter' => ['copy' => 'The obverse of this Washington quarter bears John Flanagan\'s familiar portrait of America\'s first president. Its reverse features his depiction of a proud eagle with spread wings, perched on a bundle of arrows above two olive branches.', 'alt1' => 'This Bicentennial quarter celebrates the 200th anniversary of America\'s founding with a special reverse by Jack L. Ahr, which depicts a military drummer and a victory torch encircled by 13 stars. The obverse bears John Flanagan\'s original design with dual date 1776-1976.', 'note' => 'Washington Quarters, coined since 1932, honor the first President\'s legacy. John Flanagan\'s design features George Washington on the obverse, with the nation\'s motto "In God We Trust." The reverse showcases an eagle with outstretched wings. These silver quarters underwent composition changes in 1965, transitioning to copper-nickel alloy. Washington Quarters connect numismatics with modern history and commemorate pivotal moments in the United States\' story.'],
    ],
];
